\begin{abstract}
LLM-as-a-judge evaluation corrects for judge bias and benchmark contamination independently, but sequential correction produces biased model ability estimates. We formalize this failure as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem: when judge severity and contamination effects are both present, any estimator that models one while omitting the other yields asymptotically biased ability estimates---even when the two facets are uncorrelated. We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly calibrates model ability, item difficulty, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility through staged estimation with externally anchored per-item exposure indicators. Formal identifiability analysis proves that fixing exposure externally is both necessary and sufficient for parameter separation under standard multi-judge designs. Error propagation analysis establishes a critical noise threshold $\sigma_{\mathrm{crit}} = \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(e_i)}$ below which ability estimates degrade by less than 6\%. In synthetic experiments ($N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$), G-MFRM reduces ability RMSE by 51.2\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$). On Arena-Hard with 8 target models and 9 judges, G-MFRM identifies per-model contamination signals (top-4 models' 95\% CIs exclude zero, though posterior CI coverage is 71\% in synthetic validation) and interpretable judge severity profiles, with nominally higher rank agreement ($\tau = 0.857$ vs.\ $0.786$; bootstrap $\Delta\tau$ CI includes zero at this sample size). LOO cross-validation provides decisive model preference for G-MFRM over standard MFRM ($\Delta\mathrm{ELPD} = -60$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 6.4$).
\end{abstract}

