\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation increasingly relies on LLM-as-a-judge protocols, where one or more LLM judges score model responses on structured benchmarks \citep{zheng2023mtbench}. Two well-documented sources of distortion affect these evaluations: \emph{judge bias}, where different judges assign systematically different scores to the same response, and \emph{benchmark contamination}, where models achieve inflated scores on items encountered during training. Both problems have received sustained research attention, producing separate toolkits for bias calibration and contamination detection. These toolkits treat the two distortions as independent phenomena, a separation that reflects their origins in different research communities rather than any principled argument for independence. Accurate ability ranking is essential for model selection, safety evaluation, and benchmark design; systematic distortion in estimated abilities undermines all three. Yet a practitioner who needs both corrections must apply them sequentially: first correct one source of distortion, then the other. This sequential approach produces unreliable ability estimates. In synthetic experiments with 50 models, 100 items, and 8 judges, sequential estimation yields ability root mean squared error (RMSE) of $0.254 \pm 0.045$, while joint estimation achieves $0.124 \pm 0.016$, a 51.2\% reduction (Wilcoxon $p < 0.001$). The gap does not arise from estimation noise or algorithmic limitations of individual methods; it reflects structural confounding between judge bias and contamination that no sequential pipeline can resolve.

Existing methods address these distortions in isolation. Judge bias corrections---CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, UDA \citep{uda2026}---remove systematic inter-judge differences without modeling contamination. Contamination detection tools---ConStat \citep{constat2024}, DCR \citep{dcr2025}, LogProber \citep{logprober2024}---adjust for memorization without accounting for judge effects (see Section~\ref{sec:related_work} for detailed review). A bias-first pipeline absorbs contamination-induced score inflation into ability estimates, because the bias model attributes all residual systematic variation to genuine ability. A contamination-first pipeline misattributes judge severity differences as contamination, because score elevation from a lenient judge is indistinguishable from elevation due to memorization without a joint model. In either ordering, the first correction stage produces estimates distorted by the unmodeled facet, and the second stage inherits this distortion.

We formalize this confounding as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem \citep{angrist2009mostly, chernozhukov2022ovb}. Within the Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) framework \citep{linacre1989mfrm}, the complete data-generating process includes both judge severity $d_j$ and a contamination interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$. Fitting a specification that omits the contamination term produces biased parameter estimates: the omission of $c_n \cdot e_i$ induces a specification bias that persists regardless of confounding structure, with an additional directional component when judge severity and contamination are non-orthogonal. The OVB framework predicts that the bias magnitude scales with the contamination susceptibility $c_n$ of each model, so the most contaminated models receive the largest upward distortion in estimated ability. For practical benchmark evaluations, this means that the more a model's training data overlaps with the benchmark, the more its ability is overestimated, precisely the failure mode that contamination correction is designed to prevent. Synthetic validation confirms the predicted pattern: the Pearson correlation between sequential estimation bias and true $c_n$ reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$), which shows that contaminated models are systematically overestimated under both sequential orderings (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which combines the multi-facet structure of MFRM \citep{linacre1989mfrm, jiao2025mfrm_llm} with the gating mechanism of Doubly-Gated Item Response Theory (DG-IRT) \citep{shu2013dgirt} to jointly estimate model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility in a single measurement framework. G-MFRM extends the MFRM linear predictor with a multiplicative contamination gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$, where $c_n \geq 0$ captures model-specific contamination susceptibility and $e_i \in [0,1]$ quantifies per-item exposure. When $c_n = 0$ or $e_i = 0$, the gating term vanishes and the model reduces to standard MFRM, so uncontaminated model-item pairs incur no distortion. Because the bilinear product $c_n \cdot e_i$ cannot be uniquely decomposed from response data alone, G-MFRM employs a three-stage calibration procedure: (1) standard MFRM warm-start to obtain initial parameter estimates, (2) external per-item exposure estimation from evidence independent of the response data, and (3) joint Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling with $e_i$ fixed from Stage~2. We provide a formal identifiability analysis (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) that proves fixing $e_i$ externally is both necessary and sufficient for parameter separation under standard multi-judge designs (Section~\ref{sec:method}). A complementary error propagation analysis bounds the impact of Stage~2 measurement noise: when exposure estimation error satisfies $\sigma \leq 0.2$, ability RMSE degradation remains below 6\%. Figure~\ref{fig:framework} provides an overview of the complete framework.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We identify and characterize, under a linear approximation to the logit link, the confounding between judge bias and benchmark contamination in LLM evaluation as an omitted variable bias problem, and show that sequential correction produces directional bias proportional to model contamination levels (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}, Figure~\ref{fig:ovb_bias}).
    \item We propose the G-MFRM framework with formal identifiability guarantees (Propositions~\ref{prop:no_new}--\ref{prop:exposure_variation} and Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) and quantified error propagation bounds for the staged estimation procedure (Section~\ref{sec:method}, Table~\ref{tab:error_prop}).
    \item Synthetic validation confirms that joint estimation reduces ability RMSE by 51.2\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$), with Kendall rank correlation $\tau = 0.922$ versus $\tau = 0.839$ (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
    \item On Arena-Hard with 9 LLM judges and 8 target models, G-MFRM produces the first joint diagnostic of per-model contamination susceptibility and per-judge severity, with nominally improved rank agreement ($\tau = 0.857$ vs.\ $0.786$ for all seven baselines; Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Confounding in LLM Evaluation}
\label{sec:confounding}

The Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) extends classical Rasch measurement to settings with multiple rater facets \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm}. Originating in educational measurement, the model treats rater variation as a nuisance facet to be estimated and removed rather than a source of noise to be averaged over. In the LLM evaluation context, where $J$ judges score $N$ models across $I$ items, the MFRM specifies
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mfrm}
\log \frac{P(Y_{nij} \geq k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)}{P(Y_{nij} < k \mid \theta_n, b_i, d_j, \tau_k)} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k,
\end{equation}
%
where $Y_{nij}$ is the ordinal rating by judge $j$ for model $n$ on item $i$, $\theta_n$ is the ability of model $n$ ($n = 1, \ldots, N$), $b_i$ is the difficulty of item $i$ ($i = 1, \ldots, I$), $d_j$ is the severity of judge $j$ ($j = 1, \ldots, J$), and $\tau_k$ is the threshold for category $k$. All facets reside on a common logit scale, so model ability is directly comparable after adjustment for item difficulty and judge severity. \citet{jiao2025mfrm_llm} applied this framework to compare ten LLM raters with human experts, showing that MFRM separates genuine ability from systematic judge effects in multi-judge panels.

However, standard MFRM assumes that all systematic sources of score variation are captured by the modeled facets. This assumption breaks down when some models have been exposed to benchmark items during training \citep{dcr2025}. Such contamination inflates scores on exposed items through memorization rather than genuine ability. We represent this effect as an interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$, where $c_n \geq 0$ is the contamination susceptibility of model $n$ and $e_i \in [0, 1]$ is the exposure level of item $i$. The complete data-generating process becomes
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:full}
\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i.
\end{equation}
%
When a practitioner fits the standard MFRM (Eq.~\ref{eq:mfrm}) to data generated by Eq.~\ref{eq:full}, the contamination term is absent from the estimated model. Its influence does not vanish but is absorbed into the estimated parameters $\tilde{\theta}_n$, $\tilde{b}_i$, and $\tilde{d}_j$, which distorts all downstream inferences.

The consequence of this omission can be stated precisely.

\begin{observation}[Omitted Variable Bias in MFRM]
\label{obs:ovb}
Let the full model be $\eta_{nijk} = \theta_n - b_i - d_j - \tau_k + c_n \cdot e_i$ and the short model omit the contamination term: $\tilde{\eta}_{nijk} = \tilde{\theta}_n - \tilde{b}_i - \tilde{d}_j - \tau_k$. Under a linear approximation to the ordinal logit link \citep{wooldridge2010, angrist2009mostly},\footnote{The OVB formula is exact for linear models. We apply it to the MFRM linear predictor $\eta$, which is an approximation. Synthetic validation confirms the approximation quality: the Pearson correlation between the predicted bias direction and empirical bias reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$), indicating that the linear result accurately characterizes bias direction and relative magnitude.}
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ovb}
\mathrm{plim}(\tilde{\theta}_n - \theta_n) = \gamma_n \cdot c_n,
\end{equation}
where $\gamma_n$ depends on the projection of the exposure vector $(e_i)$ onto the design matrix of the short model (explicit form in Appendix~\ref{app:gamma}). The bias is non-zero whenever $\mathrm{Cov}(d_j,\, c_n \cdot e_i) \neq 0$. This captures the confounding component; in the nonlinear MFRM, a specification bias from omitting $c_n \cdot e_i$ persists even at zero covariance (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{observation}

The bias combines a specification component from omitting $c_n \cdot e_i$, which persists regardless of confounding, with a confounding component from non-orthogonality between $d_j$ and $c_n \cdot e_i$. Monte Carlo simulation confirms that bias magnitude is approximately constant across confounding levels $\rho \in \{0, 0.3, 0.6, 0.9\}$, indicating that the omission of $c_n \cdot e_i$---not its correlation with $d_j$---is the primary driver (Figure~\ref{fig:bias_vs_rho}).

This confounding undermines both sequential estimation strategies. A bias-first approach, as in CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026}, calibrates $d_j$ before estimating ability, but the resulting $\hat{\theta}$ is already inflated for contaminated models because the omitted $c_n \cdot e_i$ term has been absorbed into the ability estimates. A contamination-first approach, as in DCR \citep{dcr2025}, adjusts for contamination before addressing judge effects, but judge severity is then misattributed as contamination signal. Synthetic validation confirms this failure: with $N = 50$ models, $I = 100$ items, $J = 8$ judges, and 20 random seeds, the Pearson correlation between sequential estimation bias and true $c_n$ reaches $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$). As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:ovb_bias}, the bias is not random but directional: contaminated models are systematically overestimated under both sequential orderings.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig2_ovb_bias}
\caption{Sequential estimation bias ($\hat{\theta}_{\mathrm{seq}} - \theta_{\mathrm{true}}$) versus true contamination susceptibility $c_n$. Pearson $r = 0.86$ ($p < 0.001$) confirms systematic overestimation of contaminated models. Synthetic data ($N = 50$, $I = 100$, $J = 8$, 20 seeds).}
\label{fig:ovb_bias}
\end{figure}

